From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Il tuo nome <tu@esempio.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 16:31:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nb/intel/haswell: Reset the setup options only when the CMOS
 was lost too

The setup options are reset when RTC_PWR_STS says that the RTC lost
power. On Lynx Point that bit, GEN_PMCON_3 bit 2, is read-write: on a
ThinkPad W541 under Linux, writing 1 to it reads back 1 (0x1808 ->
0x180c) and writing 0 clears it. The OS, or any root process, could
then reset every option at the next boot.

With CMOS_POST, which the deletion requires here, also require that the
CMOS lost its contents: the bootblock then finds no valid bank mark and
clears both banks (cmos_post_init()), so the previous boot reads as
code 0 with no extra. A real power loss of the RTC well does that; the
bit set by software leaves the code of the boot before (usually
POSTCODE_OS_BOOT). The boot after a deletion that was interrupted by a
reset before ramstage clears the bit, the hang the code already skips
or the reset of an option, finds a code there and does not delete again.

Clear a bit set without a lost CMOS in romstage: sb_rtc_init() would
take it for a power loss and zero the CMOS, CMOS_POST included
(cmos_init() without USE_OPTION_TABLE), so that the next boot would find
the CMOS lost, and a bit set once more would reset the options. A real
loss loses the bank mark too, and keeps the bit for sb_rtc_init().

Software that also rewrites the CMOS bytes of CMOS_POST can still do
it, and a power loss that leaves a valid bank mark by chance keeps the
options (the next one resets them): the check is against a stray bit,
not against root.

TEST=On a ThinkPad W541, before the clear of a stray bit: the bit set
from Linux alone and a reboot reset nothing; with the CMOS_POST bytes
also cleared, the 66 options were reset. The clear built only.

Change-Id: I7741bf77b808abfb59702b3f75940ff72efa70dd
---
 src/northbridge/intel/haswell/romstage.c | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/northbridge/intel/haswell/romstage.c b/src/northbridge/intel/haswell/romstage.c
index 30e54bb985..bb63ab86e4 100644
--- a/src/northbridge/intel/haswell/romstage.c
+++ b/src/northbridge/intel/haswell/romstage.c
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@ static enum {
 	OPTIONS_RESET,
 	OPTIONS_NOT_RESET,
 	OPTIONS_RESET_SKIPPED,
+	OPTIONS_RESET_NOT_CMOS,
 } options_reset;
 
 /*
@@ -39,8 +40,16 @@ static enum {
  * failure is never a resume, but a resume must not write the flash.
  *
  * RTC_PWR_STS is left as it is: sb_rtc_init() in ramstage logs the ELOG event,
- * clears it and resets the CMOS, as before. A reset before ramstage repeats the
- * deletion, which then finds nothing more; a refused store is not tried again.
+ * clears it and resets the CMOS, as before. A refused store is not tried again.
+ *
+ * On Lynx Point the OS can set RTC_PWR_STS too (GEN_PMCON_3 bit 2 is read-write,
+ * checked on a ThinkPad W541): the bit alone would let software reset every
+ * option at the next boot. With CMOS_POST, which Kconfig requires here, the
+ * deletion also needs the CMOS to have lost its contents: the bootblock then
+ * found no valid bank mark and cleared both banks (cmos_post_init()), so the
+ * previous boot reads as code 0 with no extra. Any boot that ran before, and
+ * any reset before ramstage cleared the bit (the deletion that hung, or a reset
+ * of an option after it), left a code there, so it is not repeated either.
  */
 static void reset_options_on_rtc_failure(void)
 {
@@ -66,6 +75,22 @@ static void reset_options_on_rtc_failure(void)
 			options_reset = OPTIONS_RESET_SKIPPED;
 			return;
 		}
+		if (code || extra) {
+			printk(BIOS_WARNING, "Setup options: not reset, RTC power lost but the "
+			       "CMOS kept the last boot (POST 0x%02x/0x%08x)\n", code, extra);
+			options_reset = OPTIONS_RESET_NOT_CMOS;
+			/*
+			 * Clear the stray bit here: sb_rtc_init() would take it for a
+			 * power loss and zero the CMOS, CMOS_POST included, so that the
+			 * next boot finds the CMOS lost, and the bit set once more
+			 * resets the options. A real loss loses the bank mark too, and
+			 * keeps the bit. Bits 1, 9 and 14 are write-1-to-clear: 0 here.
+			 */
+			u16 pmcon3 = pci_read_config16(PCH_LPC_DEV, GEN_PMCON_3);
+			pmcon3 &= ~(RTC_BATTERY_DEAD | PWR_FLR | GEN_RST_STS | SUS_PWR_FLR);
+			pci_write_config16(PCH_LPC_DEV, GEN_PMCON_3, pmcon3);
+			return;
+		}
 	}
 
 	post_code(POST_OPTIONS_RESET);
@@ -81,6 +106,9 @@ static void log_options_reset(void)
 {
 	if (options_reset == OPTIONS_RESET)
 		printk(BIOS_INFO, "RTC power lost: setup options reset to defaults\n");
+	else if (options_reset == OPTIONS_RESET_NOT_CMOS)
+		printk(BIOS_WARNING, "RTC power lost: setup options not reset, the CMOS kept "
+		       "its contents\n");
 	else if (options_reset != OPTIONS_KEPT)
 		printk(BIOS_ERR, "RTC power lost: setup options not reset%s\n",
 		       options_reset == OPTIONS_RESET_SKIPPED ? " (last boot hung there)" : "");
-- 
